        {
          "id": "note-wire",
          "type": "Note",
          "position": {
            "x": 307.1534139592691,
            "y": -112.87437388469846
          },
          "width": 360,
          "height": 150,
          "config": {
            "text": "SERIAL needs a link to move bytes. This example self-runs over a com0com virtual pair (COM3<->COM4): 'serial-tx' writes COM3, 'serial-rx' reads COM4. For real hardware, point both at your device(s), or use a single Serial.Port with its TX and RX pins shorted (hardware loopback). The Sources.Iterator drives Bytes.Pack directly, so one 4-byte record is emitted per step and the counter advances by exactly one each frame (no clock drift)."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "note-framers",
          "type": "Note",
          "position": {
            "x": 858.2911747146641,
            "y": -38.20659057651923
          },
          "width": 380,
          "height": 200,
          "config": {
            "text": "This record [len=3, count, 0x2A, 0x0A] can be framed several ways. This example wires Framer.Fixed (frameSize 4), which keeps the whole 4-byte record; Bytes.Unpack then reads count at byte offset 1. Swap in Framer.Delimiter (split on the 0x0A byte; yields [3, count, 0x2A], count at offset 1) or Framer.LengthPrefix (1-byte length; reads len=3 and emits [count, 0x2A, 0x0A], count at offset 0). Pick the framer matching your real protocol."
          }
        },
        {
